module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import MathlibExt.Dynamics.Ergodic.KolmogorovSinaiEntropy
public import Mathlib.Probability.ProductMeasure
public import Mathlib.Probability.UniformOn

/-!
# Kolmogorov–Sinai entropy of Bernoulli shifts and the generator theorem: wanted results

The central theorems of Sinai's entropy theory, stated with the definitions of
`MathlibExt.Dynamics.Ergodic.KolmogorovSinaiEntropy`:

* `ksEntropy_eq_entropyRel_of_isGenerator`: Sinai's generator theorem (Sinai, Scholarpedia
  *Kolmogorov–Sinai entropy*, Theorem 1; Sarig, Theorem 4.4).
* `ksEntropy_bernoulliShift`: the entropy of the Bernoulli shift with probability vector `p` is
  `-∑ pᵢ log pᵢ` (Sarig, Proposition 4.6).
* `not_isomorphic_bernoulli_uniform_three_two`: the `(1/3, 1/3, 1/3)` and `(1/2, 1/2)` Bernoulli
  schemes are not isomorphic (Sarig, Proposition 4.6). It follows from
  `ksEntropy_bernoulliShift` and the proved invariance `ksEntropy_eq_of_isomorphic`.

The Bernoulli scheme on a finite alphabet `S` with probability vector `p` is the one-sided left
shift `x ↦ (n ↦ x (n + 1))` on `ℕ → S` with the product measure `Measure.infinitePi (fun _ ↦ p)`
(Sarig, §1.5.3).
-/

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.Dynamics.KolmogorovSinaiWanted

open MeasureTheory
open MathlibExt.Dynamics.Ergodic.KolmogorovSinaiEntropy
open scoped ENNReal

/-- **Sinai's generator theorem.** Let `T` be an invertible measure-preserving transformation of
a probability space `(α, μ)` and let `ξ = {C₁, …, C_r}` be a finite measurable partition which is
a generator: the σ-algebra generated by all `Tⁿ Cⱼ`, `n ∈ ℤ`, is the whole σ-algebra modulo
`μ`. Then `h_μ(T) = h_μ(T, ξ)` (Ya. G. Sinai, *Kolmogorov–Sinai entropy*, Scholarpedia 4(3):2034,
Definition 2 and Theorem 1, proved in Sinai, Dokl. Akad. Nauk SSSR 124 (1959); O. Sarig,
*Lecture Notes on Ergodic Theory*, Definition 4.4 and Theorem 4.4, where finite partitions have
finite entropy). The integer powers are written as forward iterates of `T` and of `T.symm`. -/
theorem_wanted ksEntropy_eq_entropyRel_of_isGenerator {α : Type*} [MeasurableSpace α]
    {μ : Measure α} [IsProbabilityMeasure μ] (T : α ≃ᵐ α) (hT : MeasurePreserving T μ μ)
    {r : ℕ} {ξ : Fin r → Set α} (hξ : IsMeasurablePartition μ ξ)
    (hgen : ∀ s : Set α, MeasurableSet s → ∃ t : Set α,
      MeasurableSet[MeasurableSpace.generateFrom
        (Set.range (fun q : ℕ × Fin r => T^[q.1] ⁻¹' ξ q.2) ∪
          Set.range (fun q : ℕ × Fin r => T.symm^[q.1] ⁻¹' ξ q.2))] t ∧ t =ᵐ[μ] s) :
    ksEntropy μ T = entropyRel μ T ξ

/-- **Entropy of a Bernoulli shift.** The Bernoulli scheme on a finite alphabet `S` with
probability vector `p` has Kolmogorov–Sinai entropy `-∑_{s ∈ S} p(s) log p(s)`
(O. Sarig, *Lecture Notes on Ergodic Theory*, Proposition 4.6, where `log = log₂`; with the
natural logarithm of `partitionEntropy` both sides are rescaled by the same factor). The
computation goes through the generator theorem applied to the partition into 1-cylinders. -/
theorem_wanted ksEntropy_bernoulliShift {S : Type*} [Fintype S] [MeasurableSpace S]
    [MeasurableSingletonClass S] (p : Measure S) [IsProbabilityMeasure p] :
    ksEntropy (Measure.infinitePi fun _ : ℕ => p) (fun (x : ℕ → S) (n : ℕ) => x (n + 1)) =
      ENNReal.ofReal (∑ s, Real.negMulLog (p {s}).toReal)

/-- **Kolmogorov's non-isomorphism of Bernoulli schemes.** The `(1/3, 1/3, 1/3)` and the
`(1/2, 1/2)` Bernoulli schemes are not measure-theoretically isomorphic: there is no
measure-preserving measurable equivalence conjugating the two shifts, because their entropies
are `log 3 ≠ log 2` (O. Sarig, *Lecture Notes on Ergodic Theory*, Proposition 4.6). -/
theorem_wanted not_isomorphic_bernoulli_uniform_three_two :
    ¬ ∃ e : (ℕ → Fin 3) ≃ᵐ (ℕ → Fin 2),
      MeasurePreserving e
        (Measure.infinitePi fun _ : ℕ => ProbabilityTheory.uniformOn (Set.univ : Set (Fin 3)))
        (Measure.infinitePi fun _ : ℕ => ProbabilityTheory.uniformOn (Set.univ : Set (Fin 2))) ∧
      Function.Semiconj e (fun (x : ℕ → Fin 3) (n : ℕ) => x (n + 1))
        (fun (x : ℕ → Fin 2) (n : ℕ) => x (n + 1))

end MathlibExt.Dynamics.KolmogorovSinaiWanted
